78:  * IMPORTANT: DO NOT ENABLE THIS ON LINUX, unless you understand exactly
79:  * why I recommend against it and decide that you trust it more than my
80:  * analysis below.
81:  *
82:  * There are two versions of this code: one inside libffi itself, and
83:  * one inside malloc_closure.h here.  Both should be fine as long as the
84:  * Linux distribution does _not_ enable extra security features.  If it
85:  * does, then the code in malloc_closure.h will cleanly crash because
86:  * there is no reasonable way to obtain a read-write-execute memory
87:  * page.  On the other hand, the code in libffi will appear to
88:  * work---but will actually randomly crash after a fork() if the child
89:  * does not immediately call exec().  This second crash is of the kind
90:  * that can be turned into an attack vector by a motivated attacker.
91:  * So, _enabling_ extra security features _opens_ an attack vector.
92:  * That sounds like a horribly bad idea to me, and is the reason for why
93:  * I prefer CFFI crashing cleanly.
